Give ndbootd a first-stage boot program per client.

ndbootd already finds a client's *second*-stage program by turning its IP
address into a filename in a directory -- C0A8007C.SUN2 -- exactly as a Sun-3
PROM does when TFTPing.  The first stage, ND blocks 1-15, is one file for
every client.

Two Sun-2s can need entirely different first stages.  NetBSD's bootyy reads
the second stage from block 16 onwards and stays on ND; SunOS's sun2.bb stops
using ND after those 15 blocks and finishes over RARP and TFTP.  Serving one
of them to a machine expecting the other gets nowhere.

The open is already per-client -- ndbootd re-opens both programs whenever its
client cache expires -- so this is only about the name.  The trailing argument
may now be a directory, and then the first stage is looked up in it under the
same per-client name the second stage uses.  A plain filename behaves exactly
as before.

